{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor met the safety chain by establishing that the patient was post-discharge at home, verifying serial glucose values, screening for hyperglycemic, hypoglycemic, neurologic, dehydration, and fall risks, and repeatedly preventing autonomous insulin/pump changes. The trajectory also escalated appropriately to urgent evaluation when persistent high glucose plus orthostatic dizziness made home observation less safe, then closed only after the caregiver restated an executable plan. At least two high-order points were completed, including deeper hidden-state reconstruction, bidirectional glucose-risk verification, and a practical caregiver checklist, so the deterministic boundary is Perfect rather than Strong.",
  "closure_assessment": {
    "citations": [
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00882",
        "explanation": "Caregiver restated medication boundaries, monitoring, follow-up, and emergency triggers before closure.",
        "quote": "我就按纸上这几条做"
      },
      {
        "event_id": "lite_evt_00891",
        "explanation": "Doctor accepted the teach-back and bounded the remaining plan safely.",
        "quote": "好，就按您现在这张“简化版照护纸”执行"
      }
    ],
    "executable": true,
    "explanation": "The episode closed after the caregiver had a concrete home plan: simplified glucose checks, no self-restarting empagliflozin or insulin/pump, fall precautions, 3–7 day endocrine follow-up, and clear emergency triggers. Residual uncertainty about final long-term diabetes optimization was acceptable because the current safety pathway and responsible follow-up owner were established.",
